president biden spent much of this week  focusing more on foreign  than domestic policy he announced a  withdrawal date for u.s troops in  afghanistan and sanctions against  russia for its acts of aggression  against the u.s  christina raffini is at the white house  christina good morning to you  good morning jeff as you mentioned since  coming to office president biden has  really tried to focus on domestic issues  right  covet economic relief infrastructure but  as his own secretary of state likes to  say  foreign policy is domestic policy and  this week  there was a lot of it and i'm really  pleased to welcome such a close ally  and good partner japanese prime minister  yoshihiresuga  is the first world leader to be hosted  by president biden at the white house  a show of solidarity aims at america's  biggest strategic competitor  we committed to working together to take  on the challenges from china  the biden administration wants to turn  its foreign policy  focus away from the middle east and  redeploy those resources towards  countering russia and china  we'll be much more formidable to our  adversaries and competitors over the  long term  if we fight the battles for the next 20  years  not the last 20. this week the president  announced he will pull  all u.s forces out of afghanistan by  september 11th  the president made the decision to start  the drawdown on  may first and that's the plan that we're  going on the white house  also unveiled sweeping action against  moscow  expelling 10 russian officials  sanctioning dozens of  others and officially blaming russian  intelligence services for the massive  computer hack known as solar winds  obviously we've put sanctions in place  in order to send a clear message that  there should be consequences for the  actions  but there were consequences for the  consequences with the kremlin yesterday  announcing its own sanctions  and saying it plans to expel 10 american  officials  a move the state department called  escalatory and regrettable  and there was another foreign policy  dust up over refugee policy the white  house appeared to announce it would keep  the cap for refugees allowed in the  country  at 15 000 which was an historic low set  by the previous administration  but after a lot of pushback from  democrats and human rights groups  the white house said it was reevaluating  and there would be a higher number  announced  later this month christina thank you
 

